{"name":"Rhode Island Red","hex":"#9b5b55","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Rhode Island Red","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A breed of chicken, with various subbreeds, some dual-purpose (for eggs and meat), some primarily layers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Rhode_Island_Red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rhode Island Red","blocks":[{"text":"The Rhode Island Red is an American breed of domestic chicken. It is the state bird of Rhode Island. It was developed there and in Massachusetts in the late nineteenth century, by cross-breeding birds of Oriental origin such as the Malay with brown Leghorn birds from Italy. It was a dual-purpose breed, raised both for meat and for eggs; modern strains have been bred for their egg-laying abilities. The traditional non-industrial strains of the Rhode Island Red are listed as \"watch\" by The Livestock Conservancy. It is a separate breed to the Rhode Island White."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Rhode_Island_Red_cock%2C_cropped.jpg/330px-Rhode_Island_Red_cock%2C_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhode_Island_Red"},{"title":"Rhode Island","blocks":[{"text":"Rhode Island is a state in the New England region of the Northeastern United States. It borders Connecticut to its west; Massachusetts to its north and east; and the Atlantic Ocean to its south via Rhode Island Sound and Block Island Sound; and shares a small maritime border with New York, east of Long Island. Rhode Island is the smallest U.S. state by area and the seventh-least populous, with slightly more than 1.11 million residents as of 2025. The state's population, however, has continually recorded growth in every decennial census since 1790, and it is the second-most densely populated state after New Jersey. The state takes its name from the eponymous island, though most of its land area is on the mainland. Providence is its capital and most populous city."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Flag_of_Rhode_Island.svg/330px-Flag_of_Rhode_Island.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhode_Island"},{"title":"Island","blocks":[{"text":"An island or isle is a piece of land, distinct from a continent, completely surrounded by water. There are continental islands, which were formed by being split from a continent by plate tectonics, and oceanic islands, which have never been part of a continent. Oceanic islands can be formed from volcanic activity, grow into atolls from coral reefs, and form from sediment along shorelines, creating barrier islands. River islands can also form from sediment and debris in rivers. Artificial islands are those made by humans, including small rocky outcroppings built out of lagoons and large-scale land reclamation projects used for development."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg/330px-Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Island"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Amberbock.jpg/500px-Amberbock.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Amberbock.jpg/1280px-Amberbock.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Amberbock.jpg/500px-Amberbock.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hen","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmberbock.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Poule_Rhode-Island_naine_1.jpg/330px-Poule_Rhode-Island_naine_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Poule_Rhode-Island_naine_1.jpg/330px-Poule_Rhode-Island_naine_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rhode Island Red bantam","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APoule_Rhode-Island_naine_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Nine_day_old_RIR_chick.jpg/500px-Nine_day_old_RIR_chick.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Nine_day_old_RIR_chick.jpg/500px-Nine_day_old_RIR_chick.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chick, 9 days old","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANine_day_old_RIR_chick.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Alonzo_Chappel_-_The_Landing_of_Roger_Williams_in_1636_-_43.003_-_Rhode_Island_School_of_Design_Museum.jpg/500px-Alonzo_Chappel_-_The_Landing_of_Roger_Williams_in_1636_-_43.003_-_Rhode_Island_School_of_Design_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"http